index 0000000..2f5854d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/perf/ghost-network.md
@@ -0,0 +1,205 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Every number below is an **estimate**. Nothing was measured in Studio for this
+change; the per-message byte costs come from Studio measurements of remotes
+and replicated properties (October 2026), and the rates come from the code.
+"How to measure it" at the end says how to confirm them.
+
+## How it worked before
+
+- `GhostPreview.client` fired the reliable `MoveGhost` RemoteEvent from
+  `RenderStepped`: one call per rendered frame, for **every** player, building
+  or not (placing is on by default, and a disabled placer still sent
+  `nil, nil` every frame).
+- `GhostPreview.server` moved one replicated Part per player in
+  `Workspace.Ghosts`. Each change to its `CFrame`, `Color`, `Material`, or
+  `Transparency` replicated to all 12 clients, the builder included, so your
+  own ghost trailed your cursor by a full round trip.
+
+## How it works now
+
+- Each client draws **every** ghost locally, in a client-only
+  `Workspace.Ghosts` folder. Your own ghost moves every frame from your own
+  raycast, with no network round trip.
+- A client sends its ghost only when the cell or block type changes, at most
+  **20 times a second**, plus one repeat a second while nothing changes (this
+  heals dropped packets and fills in players who joined later).
+- The message is a 9-byte buffer (`Shared/GhostPacket`): a u16 sequence
+  number, a u8 block index (0 = hidden), and three i16 grid-cell coordinates.
+- It travels over an `UnreliableRemoteEvent` (`GhostUpdate`), so it never
+  queues in front of block placement or other reliable traffic, and a stale
+  update is simply dropped. Receivers discard anything older than the last
+  sequence number they saw.
+- The server keeps no ghost parts. It checks the packet (length, block index),
+  rate-limits it with a token bucket (burst 10, 25 per second), and forwards
+  it with `FireClient` to every **other** player.
+- Receivers glide other players' ghosts toward the newest cell (exponential
+  smoothing, 0.04 s time constant). A ghost that was hidden or is more than 8
+  cells away jumps instead.
+
+## Unit costs
+
+From the `roblox-performance` skill's measured tables:
+
+| Item | Bytes | Source |
+|---|---|---|
+| Remote call, no arguments | 9 | measured |
+| `Vector3` argument | 13 (22 per call minus 9) | measured |
+| Short string argument ("Brick", "Grass") | about 7 (10 characters cost 12) | measured, scaled |
+| Buffer argument, 9 data bytes | about 12 (9 + a header of at least 2 bytes; a 16-byte buffer measured 19) | measured, scaled |
+| `Player` instance argument | about 6 | **assumed**, not measured |
+| One replicated `CFrame` change on an anchored part | 25 | measured |
+
+Several writes to the same property in one server frame replicate as one, so
+the old ghost part replicated at most once per server frame (60 Hz).
+
+**Per-message sizes:**
+
+- Before, `MoveGhost` upstream: 9 + 7 + 13 = **about 29 bytes**.
+- After, `GhostUpdate` upstream: 9 + 12 = **about 21 bytes**.
+- After, `GhostUpdate` downstream (sender `Player` + buffer): 9 + 6 + 12 =
+  **about 27 bytes**.
+
+## Scenario
+
+12 players. 4 are actively building (cursor sweeping, cell changing on every
+frame the server or client can send). 8 are idle, with the cursor resting on
+the ground. Frame rates: the 4 builders run at 240 FPS, the 8 idle players at
+60 FPS. These match the playtest that showed 300 to 400 ms ping.
+
+## Client to server
+
+**Before**: one 29-byte reliable call per rendered frame, building or not.
+
+| Player | Calls/s | KB/s |
+|---|---|---|
+| Builder at 240 FPS | 240 | 240 x 29 = 6.96 |
+| Idle at 60 FPS | 60 | 60 x 29 = 1.74 |
+| **Server receives** (4 builders + 8 idle) | 4 x 240 + 8 x 60 = **1,440** | 4 x 6.96 + 8 x 1.74 = **41.8** |
+
+**After**: at most 20 changes a second, plus one repeat a second; frame rate no
+longer matters.
+
+| Player | Calls/s | KB/s |
+|---|---|---|
+| Builder | at most 20 | 20 x 21 = 0.42 |
+| Idle | 1 | 1 x 21 = 0.02 |
+| **Server receives** | 4 x 20 + 8 x 1 = **88** | 4 x 0.42 + 8 x 0.02 = **1.85** |
+
+About **23 times fewer bytes** and **16 times fewer calls**. A 240 FPS builder
+now sends 20 calls a second instead of 240, all off the reliable channel.
+
+## Server to each client
+
+**Before**: one replicated part per player, and every client received all 12,
+its own included. How much an idle ghost cost depends on whether the engine
+sends a property write that sets the same value it already had. The old server
+wrote `CFrame`, `Color`, `Material`, and `Transparency` on every message, so
+both cases are shown.
+
+- Each building ghost: its `CFrame` changes up to once per server frame,
+  60 x 25 = **1.5 KB/s**.
+- Each idle ghost: 0 if same-value writes are skipped; up to 60 x 25 = 1.5 KB/s
+  if they are sent. (Not measured here.)
+
+| | Same-value writes skipped | Same-value writes sent |
+|---|---|---|
+| Per client | 4 x 1.5 = **6.0 KB/s** | 12 x 1.5 = **18 KB/s** |
+| Server sends, all 12 clients | **72 KB/s** | **216 KB/s** |
+
+All of it reliable and in order, sharing the queue with block placement,
+character movement, and every other replicated change.
+
+**After**: each client receives the other 11 players' packets.
+
+| Ghost | KB/s per receiving client |
+|---|---|
+| Building | at most 20 x 27 = 0.54 |
+| Idle | 1 x 27 = 0.03 |
+
+| | KB/s |
+|---|---|
+| An idle player's client (4 building + 7 idle) | 4 x 0.54 + 7 x 0.03 = **2.35** |
+| A builder's client (3 building + 8 idle) | 3 x 0.54 + 8 x 0.03 = **1.86** |
+| Server sends, all 12 clients (4 x 11 building + 8 x 11 idle) | 44 x 0.54 + 88 x 0.03 = **26.4** |
+
+That is **2.5 to 7.7 times less** per client than before, depending on the
+same-value question, and it is unreliable traffic that cannot hold up anything
+else.
+
+**Where the saving is small.** For a cursor that crosses fewer than 20 cells a
+second, both designs send one message per cell change, and a 27-byte message
+costs about the same as a 25-byte property change. The downstream saving comes
+from fast sweeps (capped at 20 instead of 60 per second), from idle ghosts
+(1 a second), and from no longer sending your own ghost back to you. The
+biggest wins are upstream and in getting ghost traffic off the reliable
+channel.
+
+## Scaling to more players
+
+Ghost traffic per client grows with the number of other builders; the
+server's total send grows with players squared.
+
+| Players (one third building) | Before, per client | After, per client | After, server total |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 12 (4 building) | 6 to 18 KB/s | 2.4 KB/s | 26 KB/s |
+| 24 (8 building) | 12 to 36 KB/s | 4.8 KB/s | 110 KB/s |
+| 50 (17 building) | 26 to 75 KB/s | 10.1 KB/s | 498 KB/s |
+
+Per client is for an idle player's client: building ghosts x 0.54 plus the
+other idle ghosts x 0.03. Server total is every ghost's rate times the other
+players who receive it.
+
+Even 50 players stays far below a 50 KB/s-per-client budget. If a server ever
+fills with mostly active builders, the next levers are, in order:
+
+1. Send only to players within some distance of the ghost, and less often with
+   distance. This changes what players see (far ghosts stop or vanish), so it
+   needs a design decision.
+2. Lower `SEND_RATE` from 20 to 10. This halves the building-ghost cost, and
+   smoothing hides most of the difference.
+3. Batch every update the server forwards in one frame into one call per
+   client, which saves the 9-byte header per sender.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Your own ghost is instant.** It is drawn from your own cursor every frame
+  instead of waiting for the server. In the 12-player test it trailed by the
+  300 to 400 ms ping; now it has no delay at any ping.
+- **Other players' ghosts glide instead of jumping cell to cell.** Each one
+  slides toward its newest cell and gets most of the way there in about 0.1 s.
+  On a fast sweep it can cut a corner diagonally between cells, and it trails
+  the builder's real cursor by their ping, your ping, up to 50 ms of send
+  interval, and the glide.
+- **Big jumps still jump.** A ghost that moves more than 8 cells (32 studs) at
+  once, or reappears after being hidden, appears at its new cell without
+  sliding.
+- **Players who join see existing ghosts within about a second** instead of
+  right away, because a ghost that is not moving is only repeated once a
+  second.
+- **Rarely, another player's ghost can sit one cell off for up to a second**
+  when the last update of a movement is dropped, until the next repeat.
+- **A ghost more than about 131,000 studs from the origin is hidden from other
+  players** (the limit of a 16-bit cell index). Your own still shows. The
+  baseplate is 2,048 studs across, so this should never happen in practice.
+- **`Workspace.Ghosts` exists only on each client now**, holding that client's
+  view of every ghost. There is no server copy. No other code in the place
+  used the server's ghost parts.
+- **Placing blocks is unchanged.** `BlockPlacer` and `BlockPlacement.server`
+  were not touched, and ghost parts still have `CanQuery` off, so the
+  placement raycast ignores them.
+
+## How to measure it
+
+1. Start a local server with as many players as Studio allows (Test >
+   Clients and Servers), or a live server with a group.
+2. Have some players sweep the cursor over the ground and leave the rest idle.
+   Run the same workload on the old commit and the new one.
+3. Read `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` on the server, and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` and
+   `Stats.DataSendKbps` on a client, with `game:GetService("Stats")` in each
+   one's command bar or in the Developer Console (F9 > Network). Read both
+   Network Ping and Data Ping in the Performance Stats bar. A Data Ping far
+   above Network Ping means the reliable queue is backed up, which was the
+   symptom before.
+4. Expect the server's receive rate to fall by an order of magnitude, and
+   Data Ping to fall to about Network Ping.
